  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Why it splits this way",
      "items": [
        "The conductor keeps the database, storage, scheduler and every HTTP route.",
        "Workers hold no state, so a pool scales by adding containers.",
        "Remote mode requires shared S3 storage: workers load user modules from it directly.",
        "FUNCTION_RUNNER=local collapses the whole right side back into the conductor."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Two pools, two runtimes",
      "items": [
        "Isolate workers run queries, mutations, actions and deploy-time evaluation in V8.",
        "Node workers run \"use node\" actions in real Node.js, for npm and node builtins.",
        "The node pool is opt-in: unset FUNRUN_NODE_WORKERS and those actions stay on the conductor."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Routing and failure",
      "items": [
        "Direct routing resolves the pool over DNS; proxy routing puts Envoy in front instead.",
        "Isolate workers call back over gRPC; node workers use FUNRUN_NODE_CALLBACK_ORIGIN, and ctx.storage fetches CONVEX_CLOUD_ORIGIN directly.",
        "A pool with no healthy worker either fails fast or falls back in-process, per FUNRUN_FALLBACK.",
        "Work that may already have started is never retried unless it is pure."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
